4 0 obj
<<
  /Parent 3 0 R
  /Next 5 0 R
  /Title (1.1 You have now acquired the basics of the PHP language. To practice them, you will now create a blog web application with a simple login system and a list of blog posts. Unlike previous TypeScript and React todo list apps, which were "frontend only" apps (all the code ran in the browser, you just used a REST API to fetch data). This time, you will create a "back-end only" app, where all the code runs on the server with PHP and only static HTML and CSS are sent to the browser. No JavaScript is used in this app.)
  /Dest 329 0 R
>>
endobj

5 0 obj
<<
  /Parent 3 0 R
  /Next 6 0 R
  /Prev 4 0 R
  /Title (1.2 This kind of app architecture was the most common one before the rise of front-end frameworks like React, Vue or Angular. You may find it old-fashioned, but it's still widely used today for small to medium web applications, as it's simple to implement and maintain. This kind of architecture is also really similar to API backends that are really common today, so it's important to understand how it works.)
  /Dest 330 0 R
>>
endobj

6 0 obj
<<
  /Parent 3 0 R
  /Prev 5 0 R
  /Title (1.3 With this app, you will learn how to upload images with an HTML form and PHP, how to verify user passwords securely and how to check if the user is logged in with PHP session. You'll also learn to use a relational database to store the information of your app and how to connect to it from PHP with PDO.)
  /Dest 331 0 R
>>
endobj

